+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent edit handling | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Field-aware merge would reduce coordinator workload by automatically combining disjoint field edits (e.g., one worker corrects observer names while another adds a species) and only surfacing overlapping changes. This could get more visits export-ready without manual review. However, scientific relationships often cross field boundaries in ways that are not mechanically detectable: changing a species identification should usually trigger reconsideration of its cover estimate, but the fields are independent. Merge rules would need continuous maintenance as field schemas evolve. The risk of an automated merge silently combining scientifically contradictory edits (e.g., different vegetation interpretations of the same plot) outweighs the workload reduction. Whole-visit revisions preserve each worker's complete observation as a unit. The coordinators already review submitted data for scientific correctness; comparing whole visits gives them the full context. The field-aware approach remains credible for projects where field independence is a verified invariant rather than a hopeful assumption. |
+| Revision ID assignment | Sequential integers per visit | UUIDs, timestamps, or cryptographic hashes | Sequential integers are deterministic, human-readable in diffs and logs, and compress well in storage. UUIDs would avoid coordination during assignment but lose ordering and readability. Timestamps would expose server clock skew and create ambiguity when revisions arrive in rapid succession. Cryptographic hashes of payload would enable content-addressable revisions but make the history harder to navigate and would not prevent duplicate submissions with different idempotency keys. |
+| Idempotency scope | Per-visit, indefinite validity | Global keys, time-limited keys, or no idempotency | Keys scoped per visit allow clients to use simple generation schemes (increment a local counter, namespace by visit) without global coordination. Indefinite validity avoids cache expiration edge cases — a tablet that reconnects after weeks offline can safely retry without wondering if the key aged out. The storage cost is one UUID per revision, which is trivial compared to the payload. Global key scope would require distributed coordination or collision risk. Time-limited keys would require clients to track expiration and regenerate, which complicates retry logic and introduces a failure mode (retry after expiration creates duplicate revisions). |
+| Unresolved visit export eligibility | Excluded from CSV export | Include with a flag, or include the most-recent revision | The Friday CSV is delivered to a regional science partner who expects reviewed data. Including unresolved visits would shift triage responsibility from coordinators (who know the field context) to the partner (who does not). Flagging unresolved visits in the export would require the partner to filter them out or interpret the flag, which violates the "export the partner's existing columns" goal. Including the most-recent revision by timestamp would be misleading — arrival order is not evidence of correctness. Exclusion makes the export semantics simple: every row has been affirmed by a coordinator, either implicitly (no conflict) or explicitly (resolved). The unresolved visit count is exposed to coordinators as a pre-export checklist. |
